\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Outline of Linear Systems of Equations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
POLS, GLS, FGLS, GMM
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Common Coefficients, Panel Data Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Preliminaries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The linear panel data model is a static model because all explanatory variables
 are dated contemporaenously with the dependent variable.
 It is also considered a 
\series bold
common coefficient model
\series default
 because 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is the same for all individuals across time.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+u_{it}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}:K\times1$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 is large and 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is small 
\begin_inset Formula $(n\ge T)$
\end_inset

.
 We assume 
\begin_inset Formula $i\neq j$
\end_inset

 are all independent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Want time heterogeneity? Then use time-dummies or the Seemingly Unrelated
 Regression (SUR) model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Want individual heterogeneity? Fixed Effects (FE) and/or Random Effects
 (RE), or something more general such as Random Coefficients (RC)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For right now, there is no individual or time heterogeneity present in the
 model.
 We will include unobserved individual heterogeneity into the panel data
 model later.
 We will also discuss mulivariate linear systems with time heterogeneity,
 i.e., the SUR model, at another time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To simplify the notation, we can stake the model over time
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=x_{i}\beta+u_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}:T\times1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}:T\times1$
\end_inset

, and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{i}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
x_{i1}^{\prime}\\
x_{i2}^{\prime}\\
\vdots\\
x_{iT}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right]_{T\times K}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
POLS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Identification Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption POLS.1:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $Ex_{it}u_{it}=0\ \forall\ i,t$
\end_inset

 (Within equation, or contemporaneous, exogeneity).
 For most applications, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 has a sufficient number of elements equal to unity, so that Assumption
 POLS.1 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i})=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This is the weakest assumption we can impose in a regression framework to
 get consistent estimators of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, and it can hold when some elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 are correlated with some elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 For example, it allows 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{is}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 to be correlated when 
\begin_inset Formula $s\neq t$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under Assumption POLS.1, the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[x_{i}^{\prime}(y_{i}-x_{i}\beta)\right]=0\ (1)$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{\prime}x_{i})\beta=E(x_{i}^{\prime}y_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 For each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vectors and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times K$
\end_inset

symmetric, positive semidefinite random matrix.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{\prime}x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is always a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times K$
\end_inset

 symmetric, postive semidefinite nonrandom matrix (the expectation here
 is define over the population distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To be able to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, we need to assume that it is the only 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vectors that satisfies (1).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption POLS.2:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $rank\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}E(x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime})\right]=K$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under Assumptions POLS.1 and POLS.2, we can write 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=\left[E(x_{i}^{\prime}x_{i})\right]^{-1}E(x_{i}^{\prime}y_{i})$
\end_inset

, which shows that the two assumptions identify the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Define the Pooled Ordinary Least Squares (POLS) estimator as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{POLS}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}y_{it}\right)=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}y_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

For computing 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{POLS}$
\end_inset

 using matrix language programming, it is sometimes useful to write 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}Y$
\end_inset

 where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X\equiv\left[\begin{array}{c}
x_{1}\\
\vdots\\
x_{n}
\end{array}\right]_{nT\times K}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y\equiv\left[\begin{array}{c}
y_{1}\\
\vdots\\
y_{n}
\end{array}\right]_{nT\times1}
\]

\end_inset

This estimator is called the 
\series bold
pooled ordinary least squares (POLS) estimator
\series default
 because it corresponds to running OLS on the observations pooled across
 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Asymptotic Properties
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Consistency
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{it}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

 by assumption, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{POLS}-\beta=\left(\frac{1}{nT}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{nT}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}u_{it}\right)\begin{array}[b]{c}
p\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}E(x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime})^{-1}E(x_{it}u_{it})=0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Asymptotic Normality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
y_{i}=x_{i}\beta+u_{i}\\
\sqrt{n}\left(\hat{\beta}_{POLS}-\beta\right)=\left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)\begin{array}[b]{c}
d\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}E(x_{i}^{\prime}x_{i})^{-1}\cdot N(0,Ex_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}x_{i})\\
V_{R}=\left(Ex_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)^{-1}(Ex_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}x_{i})\left(Ex_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\\
\hat{V}_{R}=\left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{u}_{i}\hat{u}_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)\left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)^{-1}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}=y_{i}-x_{i}\hat{\beta}_{POLS}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:R\beta=r$
\end_inset

; 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Wald:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
n(R\hat{\beta}-r)^{\prime}\left(R\hat{V}_{R}R^{\prime}\right)^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-r)\begin{array}[b]{c}
d\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\chi^{2}(K-q)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
System Conditional Homoskedasticity (SCH) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption: 
\series default

\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i})=E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By the law of iterated expectations, the SCH assumption implies that 
\series bold

\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}x_{i})=E(x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega x_{i})$
\end_inset

 
\series default
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{T\times T}\equiv E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
V_{NR}=\left(Ex_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)^{-1}(Ex_{i}^{\prime}\Omega x_{i})\left(Ex_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\\
\hat{V}_{NR}=\left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}x_{i}\right)\left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\\
\hat{\Omega}=\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum^{n}}_{i=1}\hat{u}_{i}\hat{u}_{i}^{\prime}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})=\Omega
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Homoskedasticity and No Serial Correlation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To apply the usual OLS statistics from the pooled OLS regression across
 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and for pooled OLS to be relatively efficient, we require that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 be homoskedastic across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and serially uncorrelated.
 The weakest forms of these conditions are the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption POLS.3:
\series default
 (a) 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}^{2}x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}E(x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}),\ t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}=E(u_{t}^{2})\ \forall\ t$
\end_inset

; (b) 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}u_{is}x_{it}x_{is}^{\prime})=0,\ t\neq s,\ t,s=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first part of Assumption POLS.3 is a fairly strong homoskedasticity assumptio
n; sufficient is 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}^{2}|x_{it})=E(u_{it}^{2})=\sigma^{2}\ \forall\ t$
\end_inset

.
 This means not only that the conditional variance does not depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

, but also that the unconditional variance is the same in every time period.
 Assumption POLS3(b) essentially resticts the conditional covariances of
 the errors across different time periods to be zero.
 In fact, since 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 almost always contains a constant, POLS.3(b) requires at a minimum that
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}u_{is})=0,\ t\neq s$
\end_inset

.
 Sufficient for POLS.3(b) is 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}u_{is}|x_{it}x_{is})=E(u_{it}u_{is})=0,\ \ t\neq s,\ t,s=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is important to remember that Assumption POLS.3 implies more than just
 a certain form of the unconditional variance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Assumption POLS.3 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}I_{T}$
\end_inset

, which means that the unconditional variances are constant and the unconditiona
l covariances are zero, but it also effectively restricts the conditional
 variances and covariances.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If Assumption POLS.3 holds, then 
\begin_inset Formula $AVar(\hat{\beta}_{POLS})=\sigma^{2}\nicefrac{\left[E(x_{i}^{\prime}x_{i})\right]^{-1}}{n}$
\end_inset

, so its appropriate estimator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\sigma}^{2}(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the usual OLS variance estimator from the pooled regression 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
GLS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Identification Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption SGLS.1:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $Ex_{it}u_{is}=0\ \forall\ t,s=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

 (Cross equation exogeneity, i.e., strict exogeneity).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This assumption is more easily stated using the Kronecker product, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}\otimes u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Typically, at least one element of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is unity, so in practice Assumption SGLS.1 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

SGLS.1 is stronger than POLS.1, i.e., SGLS.1 implies POLS.1.
 This stronger assumption is needed for GLS to be consistent.
 Note, GLS is less robust than POLS, but it is more effiicent than POLS
 if SGLS.1 holds and we add assumptions on the conditional variance matrix
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 A sufficient condition for Assumption SGLS.1 is the zero conditional mean
 assumption, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|x_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The second moment matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

, which is necessarily constant across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 by the random sampling assumption, plays a critical role for GLS estimation
 of systems of equations.
 Define the 
\begin_inset Formula $G\times G$
\end_inset

 positive semidefinite matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega\equiv E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 in the vast majority of applications, we will refer to 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 as the unconditional variance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Sometimes, an equation must be dropped to ensure that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is nonsingluar.
 Here, we assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular, so Assumption SGLS.1 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In place of Assumption POLS.2, we assume that a weighted expected outer product
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular.
 Here we insert the assumption of a nonsingular variance matrix for completeness.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption SGLS.2:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is positive definite and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega=C^{\frac{1}{2}}C^{\frac{1}{2}\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 (We use Cholesky or Traingular decomposition, which we can do for any symmetric
 and positive semidefinite matrix.) Sincet 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is invertible, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega^{-1}=C^{-\frac{1}{2}}C^{-\frac{1}{2}\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 The usual motivation for the GLS estimator is to transform a system of
 equations where the error has nonscalar variance-covariance matrix into
 a system where the error vector has a scalar variance-covariance matrix.
 We obtain this by premultiplying the stacked equation by 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{-\frac{1}{2}}$
\end_inset

, and then we get 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}_{i}=\tilde{x}_{i}\beta+\tilde{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}_{i}=C^{-\frac{1}{2}}y_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}_{i}=C^{-\frac{1}{2}}x_{i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{u}_{i}=C^{-\frac{1}{2}}u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Simple algebra shows that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\tilde{u}_{i}\tilde{u}_{i})=I_{T}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\series bold
generalized least squares (GLS)
\series default
 
\series bold
estimator 
\series default
of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is obtained by performing POLS of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{GLS}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{x}_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{y}_{i}\right)=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}y_{i}\right)=\left[X^{\prime}(I_{N}\otimes\Omega^{-1})X\right]^{-1}\left[X^{\prime}(I_{N}\otimes\Omega^{-1})Y\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Asymptotic Properties
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Consistency
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{GLS}-\beta=+\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}\right)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\longrightarrow
\end{array}A^{-1}E(x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i})=0
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $A=\left[E(x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}x_{i})\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If we are willing to make the zero conditional mean assumption, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{GLS}$
\end_inset

 can be shown to be unbiased conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 Note, consistency fails if we only make Assumption POLS.1.
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{\prime}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 does not imply 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 If Assumption POLS.1 holds but Assumption SGLS.1 fails, the transformation
 equation 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}_{i}=\tilde{x}_{i}\beta+\tilde{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 generally induces correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Asymptotic Normality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{n}(\hat{\beta}_{GLS}-\beta)=\left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

By the CLT,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}\right)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\longrightarrow
\end{array}N(0,B)
\]

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $B\equiv E(x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}x_{i})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}\right)=O_{p}(1)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}x_{i}\right)^{-1}-A^{-1}=o_{p}(1)$
\end_inset

, we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{n}(\hat{\beta}_{GLS}-\beta)=A^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}\right)+o_{p}(1)
\]

\end_inset

It follows from the asymptotic equivalence lemma that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{n}(\hat{\beta}_{GLS}-\beta)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{a}\\
\sim
\end{array}N(0,A^{-1}BA^{-1})$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V_{R}=\left(E\tilde{x}_{i}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(E\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{u}_{i}\tilde{u}_{i}^{\prime}\tilde{x}_{i}\right)\left(E\tilde{x}_{i}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\iff AVar(\hat{\beta}_{GLS})=\nicefrac{A^{-1}BA^{-1}}{n}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
SE:
\series default
 Use the robust standard error for POLS of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Feasible Generalized Least Squares (FGLS)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Asymptotic Properties
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Obtaining the GLS estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{GLS}$
\end_inset

 requires knowing 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 up to scale.
 That is, we must be able to write 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega=\sigma^{2}C$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 is a known 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 positive definite matrix and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 is allowed to be an unknown constant.
 Sometimes 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 is known, but much often it is unknown.
 Therefore, we now turn to the analysis of feasible GLS (FGLS) estimation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In FGLS estimation, we replace the unknown matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 with a consistent estimator.
 Because the estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 appears highly nonlinearly in the expression for the FGLS estimator, deriving
 finite sample properties of FGLS is generally difficult.
 The asymptotic properties of the FGLS estimator are easily established
 as 
\begin_inset Formula $n\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 because its first-order asymptotic properties are identical to those of
 the GLS estimator under Assumptions SGLS.1 and SGLS.2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We initially assume we have a consisten estimator, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Omega},$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \mbox{p}\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}}\hat{\Omega}=\Omega$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is allowed to be a general positive definite matrix, the following estimation
 approach can be used.
 First, obtain the POLS estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, which we denote 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
 We already showed that 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is consistent for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 under Assumptions POLS.1 and POLS.2, and therefore under Assumptions SGLS.1
 and POLS.2.
 So, a natural estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\Omega}\equiv\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\check{u}_{i}\check{u}_{i}^{\prime}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{u}_{i}\equiv y_{i}-x_{i}\check{\beta}$
\end_inset

 are the POLS residuals.
 We can show that this estimator is consistent for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 under Assumptions SGLS.1 and SOLS.2 and standed moment conditions.
 Given 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Omega},$
\end_inset

 the 
\series bold
feasible GLS (FGLS) estimator 
\series default
of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{FGLS}=\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}x_{i}\right]^{-1}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}y_{i}\right]=\left[X^{\prime}\left(I_{N}\otimes\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\right)X\right]^{-1}\left[X^{\prime}\left(I_{N}\otimes\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\right)Y\right]
\]

\end_inset

We already know that GLS is consitent and asymptotically normal.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 converges to 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

, it is not surprising that FGLS is consistent, and we can also verify that
 FGLS has the same limiting distribution of GLS, i.e., they are 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{n}$
\end_inset

-equivalent.
 This asymptotic equivalence is important because we not have to worry that
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 is an estimator when performing asymptotic inference about 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 using 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FGLS}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the FGLS context, a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{A}\equiv\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}x_{i}
\]

\end_inset

A consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is also readily available after FGLS estimation.
 Define the FGLS residuals by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}\equiv y_{i}-x_{i}\hat{\beta}_{FGLS}$
\end_inset

.
 Using standard arguements, a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{B}\equiv\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\hat{u_{i}}\hat{u}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}x_{i}}
\]

\end_inset

The estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $AVar(\hat{\beta})$
\end_inset

 can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\hat{A}^{-1}\hat{B}\hat{A}^{-1}}{n}$
\end_inset

.
 This is the extension of the White heteroskedasticity-robust asymptotic
 variance estimator, and it is robust under Assumptions SGLS.1 and SGLS.2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
System Conditional Homoskedasticity (SCH) Assumption
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under the assumptions so far, FGLS has nothing to offer over POLS, and it
 is less robust.
 However, under an addition assumption, FGLS is asymptotically more efficeint
 that POLS (and other estimators).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption SGLS.3: 
\series default

\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i})=E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})=\Omega$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The SCH assumption puts restrictions on the conditional variances and covariance
s of elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|x_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, then this assumption is the same as assuming 
\begin_inset Formula $Var(u_{i}|x_{i})=Var(u_{i})=\Omega$
\end_inset

.
 Another way to state this assumption is 
\begin_inset Formula $B=A$
\end_inset

, so this simplifies the asymptotic variance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By the law of iterated expectations, the SCH assumption implies that 
\series bold

\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}x_{i})=E(x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}x_{i})$
\end_inset

 
\series default
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{T\times T}\equiv E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Note, we only need the weaker condition to determine the usual variance
 matrix for FGLS.
 Under this weaker assumption, along with Assumptions SGLS.1 and SGLS.2, the
 asymptotic variance of the FGLS estimator is 
\begin_inset Formula $AVar(\hat{\beta})\equiv\nicefrac{A^{-1}}{n}$
\end_inset

.
 We obtain an estimator of this variance matrix by using our consistent
 estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{AVar(\hat{\beta}})=\nicefrac{\hat{A}^{-1}}{n}$
\end_inset

.
 This is the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

usual
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 formula for the asymptotic variance of FGLS.
 It is nonrobust in the sense that it relies on homoskedasticity assumption.
 If heteroskedasticity in 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is suspected, then the robust estimator, which was derived earlier, should
 be used.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under Assumptions SGLS.1, POLS.2, SGLS.2, and SGLS.3 the FGLS estimator is more
 efficeint that the POLS estimator.
 We can actually say much more: FGLS is more efficient than any other estimtator
 that uses the orthogonality conditions 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}\otimes u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Summary of the Various System GMM Estimators
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Preliminaries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+u_{it}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vector.
 Suppose we have a 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of instruments 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{it}$
\end_inset

, so the number of instruments can vary with time.
 The instruments must satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{it}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Stacking the equations over 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=x_{i}\beta+u_{i}
\]

\end_inset

which is the same setup up as in (2), and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 has the structure of (4).
 Thus, the moment conditions are given by:
\series bold
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(z_{i}^{\prime}[y_{i}-x_{i}\beta])=E(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i})=E(g_{i})_{L\times1}=0
\]

\end_inset


\series default
The efficient GMM estimator that uses only the moments 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{it}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, is the GMM estimator with optimal weighting matrix.
 However, the choice of instrument matrix in (5) means we are only using
 the moment conditions aggregated across time, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{t=1}^{T}E(z_{it}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, to obtain the efficient GMM estimator, the matrix of instruments
 should be as in (4) because this expresses the full set of moment conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
List of estimators to deal with endogeneity are: System GMM, 3SLS, S2SLS,
 P2SLS, SIV, PIV, HT.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
1.
 GMM Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{GMM}=\arg{\displaystyle \min_{\beta}}\left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)^{\prime}\hat{W}\left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $=\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)\hat{W}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)\right]^{-1}\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}}\right)\hat{W}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}^{\prime}y_{i}\right)\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To obtain the optimal GMM esitmator, we choose
\series bold
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset


\series default
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}}\hat{W}=W\equiv\left[E(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}z_{i})\right]^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the weighting matrix for the optimal GMM estimator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{W}=\left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
2.
 3SLS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The weighting matrix used by the 3SLS estimator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{W}=\left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}z_{i}\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Omega}=\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\hat{u}_{i}\hat{u}_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The procedure on how to obtain the 3SLS estimator is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
First two stages: 
\series default
Run P2SLS to get 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Third Stage:
\series default
 Obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

 and perform system GMM estimation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 3SLS estimator is efficient under the 
\series bold
conditional homoskedasiticy assumption:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|z_{i})=E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})\equiv\Omega$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.
 S2SLS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The weighting matrix used by the S2SLS estimator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{W}=\left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

The S2SLS estimator is efficient under the conditional homoskedasticity
 assumption and when 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is spherical, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega=\sigma^{2}I_{T}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
4.
 P2SLS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the same for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}=L$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 has the structure of (5).
 The P2SLS estimator exploits the orthogonality condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(z_{i}^{\prime}u_{i})=E(z_{i1}u_{i1}+\cdots+z_{iT}u_{iT})=0
\]

\end_inset

and the conditional homoskedasticity assumption.
 So, when 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 has the structure of (5), the weighting matrix used by the P2SLS estimator
 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{W}=\left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

and the P2SLS estimator is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}=\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}y_{it}\right)
\]

\end_inset

The P2SLS is efficient under the conditional homoskedasticity assumption.
 Note, when 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{it}=x_{it}$
\end_inset

, this estimator reduces to the POLS estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
5.
 SIV
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 has the structure of (4) and 
\begin_inset Formula $L=K$
\end_inset

, then we have exactly enough IVs for the explanatory variables in the system.
 Thus, the SIV estimator is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}^{\prime}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}^{\prime}y_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
6.
 PIV
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 has the structure of (5) and 
\begin_inset Formula $L=K$
\end_inset

, then we have exactly enough IVs for the explanatory variables in the system.
 Thus, the pooled instrumental variables (PIV) estimator is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}y_{it}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Note, when 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{it}=x_{it}$
\end_inset

, this estimator reduces to the POLS estimator.
 
\end_layout

